===== licenses/gui-current/README-replace-libraries.txt =====
Replacing the LGPL libraries used by x6ng
=========================================

x6ng is closed-source. It uses these libraries under the GNU Lesser General
Public License 2.1 (or later), always as separate shared libraries. The names
below are the exact file names x6ng accepts as replacements:

  libcodec2.so.1.2          codec2, FreeDV digital voice (David Rowe and contributors)
  libsstv-denoise.so.1      SSTV ML denoiser (Hany El Imam)
  libsstv-callsign.so.1     Optional SSTV callsign OCR (Hany El Imam)
  libwefax-postproc.so.1    WeFax post-processing (Hany El Imam)
  libasound.so.2            ALSA
  libpulse.so.0             PulseAudio
  libpulse-simple.so.0      PulseAudio
  libpulsecommon-16.1.so    PulseAudio (internal part of the two above)
  libc.so.6, libm.so.6      GNU C library

You have the right to modify these libraries and to use x6ng with your
modified versions. You may also reverse-engineer x6ng to the extent needed to
debug such modifications. Nothing in the x6ng terms limits these rights.

How to do it
1. Get the corresponding source from the firmware download page
   (GPL/LGPL source archive of the release you run), or from the address in
   the written offer in this folder.
2. Build your version for the radio: ARM hard-float (armhf), glibc 2.36, the
   same soname and interface as the original. A replacement libc.so.6 or
   libm.so.6 must match the radio's own dynamic loader (glibc 2.36), which is
   part of the system image and cannot be replaced this way.
3. Switch the radio off, take out the microSD card and put it in a computer.
   Open the card's DATA partition (it is FAT, any computer reads it) and
   copy your library into the folder x6ng/lib-user/ (create it if missing).
   On the radio this folder is /mnt/x6ng/lib-user/.
   Use exactly one of the names listed above. Copy the file itself, not a
   link: links, folders and any other name are ignored. Put the card back.
4. On the radio turn on  Settings > System > User LGPL libraries.
5. Restart x6ng (or switch the radio off and on).

While the switch is on, at every start x6ng copies the listed files it finds
in /mnt/x6ng/lib-user/ into a private folder of the system and loads them
before its own copies. No other file is ever loaded from /mnt/x6ng/lib-user/:
x6ng itself, its radio library libx6radio and all the other files stay the
signed ones of the installation, and their checks stay as they are. The
folder has nothing to do with updates (/data/x6ng-update).

What you will see
While at least one of your libraries is in use, the status bar and the About
pages (on the radio and in the browser) show

    Modified LGPL libraries in use

with the names of the libraries. The log and the diagnostic report list each
replaced file with its SHA-256 checksum.

How to go back
Turn off Settings > System > User LGPL libraries and restart: the folder is
ignored again. Or delete your files from /mnt/x6ng/lib-user/ and restart.
If x6ng no longer starts with your library, put the card in a computer and
delete your file from x6ng/lib-user/ on the DATA partition, or delete the
switch's flag file x6ng/.lgpl-user-on there, then restart the radio.

A replaced library must keep the same interface (same functions and
structures). If it does not, x6ng may not work; that is not a defect of x6ng.
The SSTV model file /mnt/x6ng/sstv_dnn_20x6.bin can be replaced too (copy it
to x6ng/ on the DATA partition; it is data, not code, and needs no switch).

